Package: this2that
Version: 0.5.1
Architecture: all
Maintainer: David M. Anderson <david.anderson@excelano.com>
Depends: pandoc, python3, python3-openpyxl, python3-odf
Provides: office-convert
Breaks: office-convert (<< 0.4.0)
Replaces: office-convert (<< 0.4.0)
Section: utils
Priority: optional
Homepage: https://github.com/excelano/this2that
Description: Converters between plain-text, Office, and data file formats
 Thirteen command-line converters, each named <from>2<to>: md2docx and
 docx2md (Markdown and Word), md2pptx (Markdown to PowerPoint), docx2odt and
 odt2docx (Word and OpenDocument text), csv2xlsx and xlsx2csv (CSV and Excel),
 xlsx2ods and ods2xlsx (Excel and OpenDocument spreadsheets), sqlite2csv,
 sqlite2xlsx, and sqlite2ods (SQLite tables to CSV, Excel, and OpenDocument),
 and parquet2csv (Parquet to CSV). PowerPoint is export-only because pandoc
 writes .pptx but cannot read it.
 .
 Each command takes an input file and an optional output path, defaulting to
 the input name with the new extension. md2docx and md2pptx accept a pandoc
 reference document as a style template, csv2xlsx types cells only when the
 conversion is lossless so leading-zero codes and long digit runs survive the
 trip into Excel as text, and sqlite2csv, sqlite2xlsx, and sqlite2ods can
 each dump every table in a database in one pass (--all for sqlite2csv's
 separate CSVs, one sheet per table for the other two). xlsx2ods and
 ods2xlsx rewrite formulas between Excel syntax and OpenFormula, keeping the
 last computed value and naming the cell on stderr wherever a formula has no
 equivalent on the other side.
 .
 The converters reimplement nothing: pandoc does the Markdown, Word,
 PowerPoint, and OpenDocument text work, openpyxl does the Excel work, odfpy
 does the OpenDocument spreadsheet work, sqlite2csv uses only the Python standard library, and parquet2csv
 uses the DuckDB CLI, which Debian does not package and so is not a
 dependency: install it from https://duckdb.org/docs/installation.
